native 平台中，sprite 组件在没有 spriteFrame 时无法设置透明度值，需要在 sprite 组件更新 spriteFrame 时让其更新一次透明度值并作用到 ui 渲染上。
In native platform, sprite component can't set transparency value without spriteFrame. You need to let sprite component update transparency value once when spriteFrame is updated and apply it to ui rendering.

test bug code:

```typescript
import { _decorator, Component, Node, Sprite, SpriteFrame } from 'cc';
const { ccclass, property } = _decorator;

@ccclass('NewComponent')
export class NewComponent extends Component {

    @property(Sprite)
    testSp: Sprite = null;

    @property(SpriteFrame)
    testSpf: SpriteFrame = null;

    start() {
        this.node.on(Node.EventType.TOUCH_END, this.test, this);
    }

    test () {
        if(this.testSp){
            var targetColor = this.testSp.color.clone();
            targetColor.a = 30;
            this.testSp.color = targetColor;
            setTimeout(()=>{
                this.testSp.spriteFrame = this.testSpf;
                console.log(`target Color alpha is ${this.testSp.color.a}`);
            })
        }
    }
}
```

## Description

This PR partially reverts commit a5144e4256dc5c9d365cbbe4309a08cb15f8c7c2, reversing changes made to bba7ff8b0f2e94191258a1332eb14535d789f11e.

This change is to workaround Juju issue https://github.com/juju/juju/issues/20818 and mostly reverts back to the previous behaviour where applications would all use the same consumed offer.

To explain the issue, in https://github.com/juju/terraform-provider-juju/pull/898 we made a change to how offers are used in the integration resource. We went from multiple apps using the same consumed offer to a 1:1 relationship. See the diagrams below.

<img width="780" height="458" alt="Screenshot from 2025-10-08 12-04-24" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a18c0788-d361-4d4f-95cb-befee0a7aedc" />

This uncovered a Juju bug described in https://github.com/juju/juju/issues/20818. To solve the issue for now, I've chosen to mostly revert to the previous behaviour of the provider. This means we:
1. Relate multiple apps to the same consumed offer. (same as before)
2. Consumed offers do not have an aliased app name i.e. the offer URL `admin/dbModel.postgresql` will create a `saas` app named `postgresql`. (same as before)
3. Do not delete `saas` applications. (same as before)
4. Keep the `Update()` method empty, requiring integrations to be removed then re-created, rather than upgraded in place. (different from before)

## Type of change
- Change existing resource
- Logic changes in resources (the API interaction with Juju has been changed)


## QA steps
Using the same test plan from the previous PR 
Plan 1:
```terraform
terraform {
  required_providers {
    juju = {
      source  = "registry.terraform.io/juju/juju" # (uses local provider repository)
      version = "0.22.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "juju" {
}

resource "juju_model" "this" {
  name = "source-model"

  cloud {
    name = "localhost"
    region = "localhost"
  }
}

resource "juju_application" "source" {
  name = "dummy-source"

  model = juju_model.this.name

  charm {
    name    = "juju-qa-dummy-source"
    channel = "latest/stable"
  }
  config = {
    "token" = "abc"
  }

  units = 1
}

resource "juju_offer" "source_offer" {
  model            = juju_model.this.name
  application_name = juju_application.source.name
  name             = "dummy-source"
  endpoints        = ["sink"]
}
```

Plan 2:
```terraform
terraform {
  required_providers {
    juju = {
      source  = "registry.terraform.io/juju/juju" # (uses local provider repository)
      version = "0.22.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "juju" {
}

resource "juju_model" "this" {
	name = "sink-model"

  cloud {
    name = "localhost"
    region = "localhost"
  }
}

resource "juju_application" "sink" {
  name = "dummy-sink"

  model = juju_model.this.name

  charm {
    name     = "juju-qa-dummy-sink"
    channel  = "latest/stable"
  }

  units = 1
}

data "juju_offer" "source-offer" {
  url = "admin/source-model.dummy-source"
}

resource "juju_integration" "sink_source_integration" {
  model = juju_model.this.name

  application {
    offer_url = data.juju_offer.source-offer.url
  }

  application {
    name     = juju_application.sink.name
    endpoint = "source"
  }

}
```

Deploying the 2 models above using v0.23.0 then upgrading the provider to use this PRs changes worked fine, no changes were shown in the `sink-model`. Then what I did was,
- Comment out the block for `resource "juju_integration" "sink_source_integration"`.
- Rerun the plan to remove the integration.
- Note that the old saas app named `dummy-source-dummy-sink-source` still exists.
- Uncomment the block for `resource "juju_integration" "sink_source_integration"`.
- Reapply the plan.
- `juju show-unit <unitName>/0` and notice that the relation is not complete. We have no bumped into the Juju bug with multiple saas apps.
- Remove the relations again with Terraform (as above).
- `juju remove-saas <saas>` for both saas apps.
- Recreate the relations.
- The relation now works.

Overall, if someone upgrades to v0.23.1 with a deployment that was created with v0.23.0 and they have 2 saas apps with different names, the resolution is to remove the relations to these saas apps, remove the saas apps, and recreate the relations.

